WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
The next step forward is really with
liberal arts and graduate education

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.370
which are 19th century creations largely. There's greater inclusion.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:13.342
There's breadth. But also specialization.

00:00:13.342 --> 00:00:16.170
This is the era of the classic education.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.180
Where every young man of a certain class

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.970
had to learn Greek and Latin, but also

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.832
natural science and philosophy.

00:00:25.832 --> 00:00:34.470
But they were still very exclusive institutions
in terms of gender, race, class.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.880
So this is Rosa Luxemburg, one of my great heroines.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.820
How many of you have ever heard of Rosa Luxemburg?

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.960
My students have heard of her. One of whom is here.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:53.620
Rosa Luxemburg was a very important
political economist,

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.880
in the early part of the 20th century
...the last part of the 19th.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.040
Her death was by drowning.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.619
She was murdered by the Germans at the end of
World War I because of her social beliefs.

00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:11.585
She was a social democrat, she was a socialist, she was a communist.

00:01:11.585 --> 00:01:17.560
She fought with Lenin; had a different view of organizing.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.480
She was a  very ambitious woman

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.020
who wanted to go to graduate school.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.496
And she found... in this wonderful graphic novel called

00:01:25.496 --> 00:01:28.530
"Red Rosa" this is the image from that.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:32.310
She found that there was a graduate school that accepted women.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.750
The University of Zurich.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.173
And so she went to the University of Zurich

00:01:36.173 --> 00:01:39.739
and graduated from there with a PhD in 01887.

00:01:39.739 --> 00:01:44.970
Just to give you a sense... that it wasn't that long ago.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.460
How hard it was for women to get an education.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.658
Another heroine of mine is Gertrude Stein

00:01:50.658 --> 00:01:54.336
who went to Radcliffe. Studied with William James.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.360
So she learned psychology and philosophy.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.280
Then went to John Hopkins Medical Schoo

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.280
because it was one of the only places

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.500
a woman could go on and get a degree.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.540
She was bored there. And left to go to Paris

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.800
to become the Gertrude Stein we know.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.400
Using some of the things she learned

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.120
from William James in the process of writing her poetry.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.120
My own experience in getting an education

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.160
involved a place called Western High School

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.620
in Baltimore. It was the first public high school

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.040
that women could attend.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.980
I was not in the first class. That was in 01844.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.840
Walt Whitman gave the convocation speech.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.840
But it still exists.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.520
And it's got this very long history.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.740
It was created as a place where women could go...

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:41.020
There was an Eastern and a Western

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.200
because women couldn't travel very far

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.400
from where they lived.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.940
And it really prepared women to be teachers.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:52.040
Not University teachers, but teachers of young children.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.280
And it grew from that.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.280
So it became one of the famous places

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:56.860
for college preparatory work.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.886
A public high school, right?

00:02:58.886 --> 00:03:00.406
Others followed suit.

00:03:00.406 --> 00:03:03.200
I then chose to go to Bryn Mawr College.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.380
Which had a similar history, but for universities.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.880
Founded in 01855—

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.920
again I was not in the first class.

00:03:10.926 --> 00:03:14.950
Though I am getting close to feeling like I was.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:17.800
And it was a place that was the first

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.920
college for women that did two things.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.720
One it followed the Harvard model

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.140
in terms of standards.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.960
So it was the highest standard

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.040
of any place that women could go.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.580
And the second thing it did was provide

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.960
the first graduate program at a women's college.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.840
So it was one of the first and only places that women

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.300
could get a college--both a college education

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.360
of a very high quality and a graduate education.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:44.873
That's what formed me.

00:03:44.873 --> 00:03:46.622
So you can see why I care

00:03:46.622 --> 00:03:48.860
about these issues of exclusion.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.360
This is James Meredith at Ole Miss (in 01962).

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.398
Just to remind you

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.340
about how recently our universities were exclusive.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.700
This is not that long ago. This is the 01960s.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.320
Now there were exceptions.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.400
Harvard was founded as an Indian College.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.680
A Native American college.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.040
There were the historically black colleges
that got formed.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.780
But nonetheless until very very recently

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.760
colleges and universities in the United States,

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.120
as well as elsewhere, were extremely exclusive.

00:04:18.128 --> 00:04:21.340
They've really opened up

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.940
in the post World War II era.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.000
More than any other time.

